sec.p:
Under the CMP model, spike train variability can be partitioned into a Poisson component, which is equal to the mean, and a modulatory component, which controls the degree of overdispersion. The continuous-time formulation of the CMP model allows us to compute this partitioning for arbitrary time intervals, and thus to analytically characterize how overdispersion grows with time bin size. In the case of a non-fluctuating stimulus component fs(t)=f¯\begin{document}$f_{s}(t)=\bar{f}$\end{document}, the mean spike count in a time bin of size Δ is given by:
(7)
E[yΔ∣s]=Δ(f¯eρg2):=Δλ¯, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbb{E}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right]=\Delta\left(\bar{f} e^{\frac{\rho g}{2}}\right):=\Delta \bar{\lambda}, \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where λ\begin{document}$\lambda$\end{document} denotes the mean firing rate under the model. The spike count variance is then given by:
(8)
var[yΔ∣s]=Δλ+(Δλ)2(κ(Δ)−1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \quad \operatorname{var}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right]=\Delta \lambda+(\Delta \lambda)^{2}(\kappa(\Delta)-1)$$\end{document}
where
(9)
κ(Δ)=1Δ2∫0Δ∫0ΔeKg(t1,t2)dt1dt2, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \kappa(\Delta)=\frac{1}{\Delta^{2}} \int_{0}^{\Delta} \int_{0}^{\Delta} e^{K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)} d t_{1} d t_{2}, \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
is the average of the exponentiated covariance function exp(Kg) over all pairs of times in the interval [0, Δ]. (Note: this formula holds for any covariance function, not just the EPL covariance function we use for Kg). From these two terms, the Fano factor as a function of bin size can be computed as the ratio of variance to mean:
(10)
FF(Δ)=1+Δλ(κ(Δ)−1). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle F F(\Delta)=1+\Delta \lambda(\kappa(\Delta)-1) .$$\end{document}
This shows that the Fano Factor approaches 1 in the limit of small bin size Δ, low firing rate λ\begin{document}$\lambda$\end{document}, or when κ(Δ) approaches 1, which occurs when the variance of the gain process ρg goes to zero. Note that κ(Δ) is a decreasing function of Δ, reflecting the decay of correlations in the gain process, and it remains constant only for a gain process with infinite lengthscale (for example, Figure 1B). Thus, the Fano factor of the CMP model grows at most linearly with time bin size. Corresponding expressions for the general case, where fs(t) is a fluctuating stimulus component, are provided in the Methods and Materials (see Appendix 2 for derivations).

We start by recalling the general CMP forward model:
y(t)∣s∼PoissProc(fs(t)⋅g(t)),logfs(t)∼GP(0,Kf),Kf(s)(t1,t2)=ρf(s)exp⁡(−(t1−t2)22ℓf(s)),log⁡g(t)∼GP(0,Kg),Kg(t1,t2)=ρgexp⁡(−12|t1−t2ℓg|q). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} y(t) \mid s & \sim \operatorname{PoissProc}\left(f_{s}(t) \cdot g(t)\right), \\ \operatorname{log} f_{s}(t) & \sim \mathcal{G} \mathcal{P}\left(0, K_{f}\right), K_{f}^{(s)}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)=\rho_{f}^{(s)} \exp \left(-\frac{\left(t_{1}-t_{2}\right)^{2}}{2 \ell_{f}^{(s)}}\right), \\ \log g(t) & \sim \mathcal{G} \mathcal{P}\left(0, K_{g}\right), K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)=\rho_{g} \exp \left(-\frac{1}{2}\left|\frac{t_{1}-t_{2}}{\ell_{g}}\right|^{q}\right) .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
In our inference framework, we consider a setting with multiple repeats (n = 1, …, N) for a given stimulus condition, with data potentially collected across different stimulus conditions (s = 1, …, S). We perform inference on a uniform grid of time points (t = 1, …, T), with grid size set to dt seconds. Note that the model itself is defined in continuous time and can therefore be consistently applied to data measured at arbitrary temporal grid resolutions.

The radial basis function (RBF) kernel diagonalizes in the Fourier domain, a property previously exploited to improve computational efficiency [35, 67]. Motivated by this, we adopt a frequency-domain representation of the stimulus-driven components (f) in our inference procedure. Accordingly, we relate the time-domain components to their corresponding Fourier-domain components:
fs=B(s)ws \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}=\mathbf{B}^{(\mathbf{s})} \mathbf{w}_{\mathbf{s}}$$\end{document}
where B(s)∈RT×Ms\begin{document}$\mathbf{B}^{(\mathbf{s})} \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times M_{s}}$\end{document} (noting that typically Ms ≪ T due to pruning of unnecessary Fourier coefficients that do not substantially contribute to explaining variability in fs\begin{document}$\mathbf{f}_{s}$\end{document} [35, 67]) represents the standard real orthonormal inverse discrete Fourier transform matrix. Then, with the diagonalization of the RBF kernel [35, 67], the Fourier-transformed stimulus-driven components ws=[w1,s,w2,s,…,wMs,s]′\begin{document}$\mathbf{w}_{s}=\left[w_{1, s}, w_{2, s}, \ldots, w_{M_{s}, s}\right]^{\prime}$\end{document} are distributed as independent Gaussian random variables with the following parameters:
wm,s∼N(0,k~m(s)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle w_{m, s} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(0, \tilde{k}_{m}^{(s)}\right)$$\end{document}
where k~m(s)=2πρf(s)ℓf(s)exp⁡(−12fm(s)2ℓf(s)2)\begin{document}$\tilde{k}_{m}^{(s)}=\sqrt{2 \pi} \rho_{f}^{(s)} \ell_{f}^{(s)} \exp \left(-\frac{1}{2} f_{m}^{(s)^{2}} \ell_{f}^{(s)^{2}}\right)$\end{document}, with fm(s)\begin{document}$f_{m}^{(s)}$\end{document} denoting the mth Fourier frequency [35, 67]. Thus, we use the frequency-domain stimulus-driven components w = w1:S in place of their time-domain counterparts f, reducing computational complexity by avoiding matrix inversions and enabling pruning (Ms ≪ T). We do not apply frequency-domain inference to the gain components g, as the heavy tails of the EPL kernel do not support effective pruning.

To jointly infer the latent variables w and g and hyperparameters θ, we introduce an efficient inference procedure based on Variational Inference (VI) [38], which aims to find an approximate distribution q (w, g) for the intractable true posterior p (w, g|y, θ). In VI, the approximate posterior is first introduced into the data log-likelihood by rewriting it as [34]:
log⁡p(y∣θ)=Eq[log⁡(p(y,w,g∣θ)q(w,g)⋅q(w,g)p(w,g∣y,θ))]=Eq[log⁡p(y,w,g∣θ)q(w,g)]⏟L(q)+Eq[log⁡q(w,g)p(w,g∣y,θ)]⏟DKL(q‖p), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \log p(\mathbf{y} \mid \theta) & =\mathbb{E}_{q}\left[\log \left(\frac{p(\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g} \mid \theta)}{q(\mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g})} \cdot \frac{q(\mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g})}{p(\mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g} \mid \mathbf{y}, \theta)}\right)\right] \\ & =\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{q}\left[\log \frac{p(\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g} \mid \theta)}{q(\mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g})}\right]}_{\mathcal{L}(q)}+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{q}\left[\log \frac{q(\mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g})}{p(\mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g} \mid \mathbf{y}, \theta)}\right]}_{D_{K L}(q \| p)},\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where DKL(q||p) is the Kullback-Leibler divergence between the variational approximation and the true posterior. Since DKL(q||p) is non-negative, ℒ(q) provides a lower bound on the model evidence, and is therefore called the Evidence Lower Bound (ELBO). Maximizing the ELBO is equivalent to minimizing DKL(q||p), thus finding the variational approximation closest to the true posterior.

We assume that the variational distributions q (w, g) factorizes into independent Gaussian distributions over stimuli s = 1, …, S and trials n = 1, …, N :


q(w,g)=∏s=1S(q(ws)∏n=1Nq(gs,n))q(g~s,n)∼N(μs,n,Σs,n)andq(ws)∼N(νs,Ωs),
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} q(\mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g}) & =\prod_{s=1}^{S}\left(q\left(\mathbf{w}_{\mathbf{s}}\right) \prod_{n=1}^{N} q\left(\mathbf{g}_{\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{n}}\right)\right) \\ q\left(\tilde{\mathbf{g}}_{\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{n}}\right) & \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{s, n}\right) \text { and } q\left(\mathbf{w}_{\mathbf{s}}\right) \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\boldsymbol{\nu}_{s}, \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{s}\right),\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



where Σs,n ∈ ℝT ×T and Ωs∈RMs×Ms\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{s} \in \mathbb{R}^{M_{s} \times M_{s}}$\end{document} are full covariance matrices. Note that, by the property of linear transformations of Gaussian random variables [68], this in turn implies that the variational distributions over the time-domain stimulus-driven components are also Gaussian:


q(f~s)=q(B(s)ws)∼N(B(s)νs,B(s)ΩsB(s)′)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle q\left(\tilde{\mathbf{f}}_{\mathbf{s}}\right)=q\left(\mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})} \mathbf{w}_{\mathbf{s}}\right) \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})} \boldsymbol{\nu}_{s}, \mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})} \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{s} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})^{\prime}}\right)$$\end{document}

Accordingly, the ELBO can be derived as:
(12)
L(q)=Eq[log⁡p(y,w,g∣θ)]−Eq[log⁡q(w,g)]=−∑t,s,ndtexp⁡((B(s)νs)t+(B(s)ΩsB(s)⊤)t,t/2+(μs,n)t+(Σs,n)t,t/2)+∑t,s,nyt,s,n((B(s)νs)t+(μs,n)t)−SN2log⁡|Kg|−12∑s,ntrace(Kg−1(μs,nμs,n⊤+Σs,n))−12∑s,mlog⁡k~m(s)−12∑s,m(k~m(s))−1(νm,s2+(Ωs)m,m)+∑s,n12log⁡|Σs,n|+∑s12log⁡|Ωs|+12∑sMs+const.  egin{document}$$\displaystyle  egin{align*} \mathcal{L}(q)= & \mathbb{E}_{q}[\log p(\mathbf{y}, \mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g} \mid 	heta)]-\mathbb{E}_{q}[\log q(\mathbf{w}, \mathbf{g})] \ = & -\sum_{t, s, n} d t \exp \left(\left(\mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})}  oldsymbol{
u}_{s}ight)_{t}+\left(\mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})}  oldsymbol{\Omega}_{s} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})^{	op}}ight)_{t, t} / 2+\left( oldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}ight)_{t}+\left( oldsymbol{\Sigma}_{\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{n}}ight)_{t, t} / 2ight) \ & +\sum_{t, s, n} y_{t, s, n}\left(\left(\mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})}  oldsymbol{
u}_{s}ight)_{t}+\left( oldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}ight)_{t}ight)- rac{S N}{2} \log \left|K_{g}ight|- rac{1}{2} \sum_{s, n} \operatorname{trace}\left(K_{g}^{-1}\left( oldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}  oldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}^{	op}+ oldsymbol{\Sigma}_{s, n}ight)ight) \ & - rac{1}{2} \sum_{s, m} \log 	ilde{k}_{m}^{(s)}- rac{1}{2} \sum_{s, m}\left(	ilde{k}_{m}^{(s)}ight)^{-1}\left(
u_{m, s}^{2}+\left( oldsymbol{\Omega}_{s}ight)_{m, m}ight)+\sum_{s, n}  rac{1}{2} \log \left| oldsymbol{\Sigma}_{s, n}ight|+\sum_{s}  rac{1}{2} \log \left| oldsymbol{\Omega}_{s}ight| \ & + rac{1}{2} \sum_{s} M_{s}+	ext { const. } 	ag{12} \end{align*}$$\end{document}

First, consider the estimation of the stimulus-drive variational parameters νs and Ωs, given the estimated gain variational parameters from the previous iteration. The gradient and Hessian of Equation 12 with respect to νs can be derived as:

(13)

∇νsL=∑nB(s)⊤(ys,n−λ^s,n)−(K~(s))−1νs,∇νs2L=−∑nB(s)⊤Λ^s,nB(s)−(K~(s))−1,
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\nu}_{s}} \mathcal{L}=\sum_{n} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathbf{s})^{\top}}\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_{s, n}\right)-\left(\widetilde{\mathbf{K}}^{(s)}\right)^{-1} \boldsymbol{\nu}_{s}, \\ & \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\nu}_{s}}^{2} \mathcal{L}=-\sum_{n} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathbf{s})^{\top}} \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{s, n} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathbf{s})}-\left(\widetilde{\mathbf{K}}^{(s)}\right)^{-1}, \tag{13} \end{align*}$$\end{document}



where ys,n=[y1,s,n,y2,s,n,…,yt,s,n]⊤,λ^s,n=[λ^1,s,n,λ^2,s,n,…,λ^t,s,n]⊤,λ^t,s,n=dtexp⁡((B(s)νs)t+\begin{document}$\mathbf{y}_{\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{n}}=\left[y_{1, s, n}, y_{2, s, n}, \ldots, y_{t, s, n}\right]^{\top}, \widehat{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_{s, n}=\left[\widehat{\lambda}_{1, s, n}, \widehat{\lambda}_{2, s, n}, \ldots, \widehat{\lambda}_{t, s, n}\right]^{\top}, \widehat{\lambda}_{t, s, n}=d t \exp \left(\left(\mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})} \boldsymbol{\nu}_{s}\right)_{t}+\right.$\end{document}
(B(s)ΩsB(s)⊤)t,t/2+(μs,n)t+(Σs,n)t,t/2),Λ^s,n\begin{document}$\left.\left(\mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})} \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{s} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})^{\top}}\right)_{t, t} / 2+\left(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}\right)_{t}+\left(\boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{s, n}\right)_{t, t} / 2\right), \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{s, n}$\end{document} is a diagonal matrix with the tth diagonal entry given by λ^t,s,n\begin{document}$\widehat{\lambda}_{t, s, n}$\end{document}, and K(s)\begin{document}$\mathbf{K}^{(s)}$\end{document} is a diagonal matrix with the mth diagonal entry equal to k~m(s)\begin{document}$\tilde{k}_{m}^{(s)}$\end{document}. Further, taking derivatives in Equation 12 with respect to Ωs yields:


∇ΩsL=−12∑nB(s)⊤Λ^s,nB(s)+12(Ωs)−1−12(K~(s))−1
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{s}} \mathcal{L}=-\frac{1}{2} \sum_{n} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})^{\top}} \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{s, n} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathrm{s})}+\frac{1}{2}\left(\boldsymbol{\Omega}_{s}\right)^{-1}-\frac{1}{2}\left(\widetilde{\mathbf{K}}^{(s)}\right)^{-1}$$\end{document}



and setting this derivative to zero results in:

(14)

Ωs=(∑nB(s)⊤Λ^s,nB(s)+(K~(s))−1)−1=−(∇νs2L)−1.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \boldsymbol{\Omega}_{s}=\left(\sum_{n} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathbf{s})^{\top}} \widehat{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{s, n} \mathbf{B}^{(\mathbf{s})}+\left(\widetilde{\mathbf{K}}^{(s)}\right)^{-1}\right)^{-1}=-\left(\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\nu}_{s}}^{2} \mathcal{L}\right)^{-1} . \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Accordingly, we update the stimulus-drive variational mean νs using Newton–Raphson optimization with the gradient and Hessian in Equation 13, and subsequently update the stimulus-drive variational covariance Ωs using the Hessian as in Equation 14.

Next, we update the gain variational parameters independently for n = 1, …, N using the updated stimulus-drive variational parameters, following an equivalent Newton–Raphson procedure, with the gradient and Hessian relative to µs,n given by:
∇μs,nL=(ys,n−λ^s,n)−Kg−1μs,n,∇μs,n2L=−Λ^s,n−Kg−1, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}} \mathcal{L}=\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n}-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_{s, n}\right)-K_{g}^{-1} \boldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}, \\ & \nabla_{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}}^{2} \mathcal{L}=-\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{s, n}-K_{g}^{-1},\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
and the update rule of Σs,n given by:
Σs,n=(Λ^s,n+Kg−1)−1=−(∇μs,n2L)−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{s, n}=\left(\widehat{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}}_{s, n}+K_{g}^{-1}\right)^{-1}=-\left(\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}}^{2} \mathcal{L}\right)^{-1}$$\end{document}
Following the above derivations, we iterate between updating the stimulus-drive variational parameters and gain variational parameters until convergence. The combined iterative procedure for variational parameter inference is summarized in Algorithm 2.

While we optimize the ELBO for parameter inference, accurately and fairly comparing models requires computing the log-likelihood on held-out data. To estimate this for the CMP model, we used the importance sampling procedure described below. Note that,

(15)

log⁡(p(𝐲|s))=∑s,nlog⁡(p(𝐲s,n|s)),where,
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \log (p(\mathbf{y} \mid s))=\sum_{s, n} \log \left(p\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n} \mid s\right)\right), \qquad \mathrm{where},$$\end{document}




(16)

p(ys,n∣s)=∫p(ys,n∣gs,n,s)p(gs,n)dgs,n.
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle p\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n} \mid s\right)=\int p\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n} \mid \mathbf{g}_{s, n}, s\right) p\left(\mathbf{g}_{s, n}\right) d \mathbf{g}_{s, n} . $$\end{document}



Observing that the integral in Equation 16 is intractable, we used importance sampling [69] to approximate it. We employed the variational distribution q(g~s,n)\begin{document}$q\left(\tilde{\mathbf{g}}_{\mathbf{s}, \mathbf{n}}\right)$\end{document} as the proposal distribution,


p(ys,n∣Bs)=Eq(gs,n)[p(ys,n∣gs,n,s)p(gs,n)q(gs,n)]
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle p\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n} \mid \mathbf{B}_{s}\right)=\mathbb{E}_{q\left(\mathbf{g}_{s, n}\right)}\left[p\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n} \mid \mathbf{g}_{s, n}, s\right) \frac{p\left(\mathbf{g}_{s, n}\right)}{q\left(\mathbf{g}_{s, n}\right)}\right]$$\end{document}





≈1I∑i=1Ip(ys,n∣gs,n(i),s)p(gs,n(i))q(gs,n(i))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \approx \frac{1}{I} \sum_{i=1}^{I} p\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n} \mid \mathbf{g}_{s, n}^{(i)}, s\right) \frac{p\left(\mathbf{g}_{s, n}^{(i)}\right)}{q\left(\mathbf{g}_{s, n}^{(i)}\right)}$$\end{document}



where log⁡gs,n(i)∼N(μs,n,Σs,n)\begin{document}$\log \mathbf{g}_{s, n}^{(i)} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(\boldsymbol{\mu}_{s, n}, \boldsymbol{\Sigma}_{s, n}\right)$\end{document}. Defining:


Ls,n(i):=log⁡(p(ys,n∣gs,n(i),s))+log⁡(p(gs,n(i)))−log⁡(q(gs,n(i)))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle L_{s, n}^{(i)}:=\log \left(p\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n} \mid \mathbf{g}_{s, n}^{(i)}, s\right)\right)+\log \left(p\left(\mathbf{g}_{s, n}^{(i)}\right)\right)-\log \left(q\left(\mathbf{g}_{s, n}^{(i)}\right)\right)$$\end{document}



and applying the log-sum-exp trick for numerical stability, we obtained:


log⁡(p(ys,n∣s))≈log⁡(∑i=1Iexp⁡(Ls,n(i)−Ls,nmax))+Ls,nmax−log⁡(I)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \log \left(p\left(\mathbf{y}_{s, n} \mid s\right)\right) \approx \log \left(\sum_{i=1}^{I} \exp \left(L_{s, n}^{(i)}-L_{s, n}^{\max }\right)\right)+L_{s, n}^{\max }-\log (I)$$\end{document}



where Ls,nmax=maxiLs,n(i)\begin{document}$L_{s, n}^{\max }=\max _{i} L_{s, n}^{(i)}$\end{document}. We set I = 1000 and used the above formulation to estimate the log-likelihood of the spiking observations in the nth test trial of the sth stimulus, and finally derived the joint log-likelihood of all test data according to Equation 15.

Here we provide the general formula for partitioning variability into a Poisson component, equal to the mean, and a modulatory component, based on the CMP model in the setting where the stimulus drive fs(t) is a time-varying signal. The continuous-time formulation of the CMP model allows this partitioning to be computed for arbitrary time windows, enabling an analytic characterization of how overdispersion grows with the bin size used to count spikes. For a time bin of size Δ, the mean is given by:


mean[yΔ∣s]=eρg/2∫0Δfs(t)dt
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{mean}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right]=e^{\rho_{g} / 2} \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t$$\end{document}



and the variance is:


var[yΔ∣s]=eρg/2∫0Δfs(t)dt+eρg∫0Δ∫0Δfs(t1)fs(t2)(eKg(t1,t2)−1)dt1dt2
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \operatorname{var}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right] & =e^{\rho_{g} / 2} \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t \\ & +e^{\rho_{g}} \int_{0}^{\Delta} \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}\left(t_{1}\right) f_{s}\left(t_{2}\right)\left(e^{K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)}-1\right) d t_{1} d t_{2}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



From these expressions, we can compute the Fano factor as a function of bin size, which is simply the ratio of variance to mean:


FF(Δ)=1+eρg/2∫0Δ∫0Δfs(t1)fs(t2)(eKg(t1,t2)−1)dt1dt2∫0Δfs(t)dt
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle F F(\Delta)=1+\frac{e^{\rho_{g} / 2} \int_{0}^{\Delta} \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}\left(t_{1}\right) f_{s}\left(t_{2}\right)\left(e^{K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)}-1\right) d t_{1} d t_{2}}{\int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t}$$\end{document}



The details of the derivation are provided in the Appendix 2. To summarize statistics at a given bin size Δ over the entire observation window T, we use the following approach: we apply a sliding window of duration Δ, shift it systematically through the entire duration T, and average the computed statistics across all such windows.

All log-likelihood figures reported in this paper are expressed as log-likelihood gains of each method relative to the baseline Poisson model. The baseline Poisson model assumed spike counts across trials are generated by a Poisson process:


yt,s,n∼Poisson(λt,s)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle y_{t, s, n} \sim \operatorname{Poisson}\left(\lambda_{t, s}\right)$$\end{document}



and, to compensate for sparsity, we further assumed a Gamma prior on the firing rate λt,s ~ Gamma (α, β), where α and β are hyperparameters. Since the Gamma distribution is conjugate to the Poisson distribution [70], the posterior distribution is also Gamma-distributed:


p(λt,s|yt,s,1:N)∼Gamma(α+∑n=1Nyt,s,n,β+N)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle p \left(\lambda_{t,s}\mid y_{t,s}, 1:N \right) \sim \mathrm{Gamma} \left(\alpha+\sum_{n=1}^{N} y_{t, s, n}, \beta+N\right)$$\end{document}



Thus, we found the MAP estimate of the firing rate by the mode of the posterior Gamma distribution, given by:


λ^t,s=α+∑n=1Nyt,s,n−1β+N
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \widehat{\lambda}_{t, s}=\frac{\alpha+\sum_{n=1}^{N} y_{t, s, n}-1}{\beta+N}$$\end{document}



To fix the hyperparameters, we performed moment matching with the mean =αβ\begin{document}$=\frac{\alpha}{\beta}$\end{document} and variance =αβ2\begin{document}$=\frac{\alpha}{\beta^{2}}$\end{document}. Note further that the mean and variance conditioned on λt,s are identical for this model, resulting in a Fano factor of 1.

This model includes only the stimulus-driven components, assuming that trial-to-trial variability is fully described by the stochastic nature of the Poisson process:
y(t)∼Poisson(λt=fs(t)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle y(t) \sim \operatorname{Poisson}\left(\lambda_{t}=f_{s}(t)\right)$$\end{document}
The stimulus drive itself is modeled by a Gaussian process, similar to the CMP model:
log⁡fs(t)∼GP(0,Kf(s)),Kf(s)(t1,t2)=ρf(s)exp⁡(−(t1−t2)22ℓf(s)2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \log f_{s}(t) \sim \mathcal{G} \mathcal{P}\left(0, K_{f}^{(s)}\right), K_{f}^{(s)}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)=\rho_{f}^{(s)} \exp \left(-\frac{\left(t_{1}-t_{2}\right)^{2}}{2 \ell_{f}^{(s)^{2}}}\right)$$\end{document}
We inferred the hyperparameters and latent stimulus-driven components of this model using a simplified version of our proposed variational inference procedure. As in the baseline Poisson model, the mean and variance conditioned on the stimulus are identical, yielding a Fano factor of 1.

This model extends the modulated Poisson framework of Goris et al. [1] to continuous time. The model in Goris et al. [1] considered spike counts within a single time bin. For spiking observations from the sth stimulus presentation with t = 1, …, T time bins, we assumed that the stimulus-independent gain is independent across time bins with shared variance σgs2\begin{document}$\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document}, i.e.,
yt,s,n∼Poisson(λt,s,n),λt,s,n=gt,s,nft,sdt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} y_{t, s, n} & \sim \operatorname{Poisson}\left(\lambda_{t, s, n}\right), \\ \lambda_{t, s, n} & =g_{t, s, n} f_{t, s} d t,\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where gt,s,n ~ Gamma(rs, ss), with shape parameter rs=1σgs2\begin{document}$r_{s}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}}$\end{document} and scale parameter ss=σgs2\begin{document}$s_{s}=\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document}. Marginalizing over the gain variables yields a product of independent Negative Binomial distributions:
(17)
p(y1:T,s,1:N∣rs,ss)=∫0∞p(y1:T,s,1:N,λ1:T,s,1:N∣rs,ss))dλ1:T,s,1:N=∏t,n=1T,NNegativeBinomial(yt,s,n∣rs,11+st,s) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} p\left(y_{1: T, s, 1: N} \mid r_{s}, s_{s}\right) & \left.=\int_{0}^{\infty} p\left(y_{1: T, s, 1: N}, \lambda_{1: T, s, 1: N} \mid r_{s}, s_{s}\right)\right) d \lambda_{1: T, s, 1: N} \\ & =\prod_{t, n=1}^{T, N} \text { NegativeBinomial }\left(y_{t, s, n} \mid r_{s}, \frac{1}{1+s_{t, s}}\right) \tag{17} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where st,s=σgs2ft,sdt\begin{document}$s_{t, s}=\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2} f_{t, s} d t$\end{document}. For the Goris-indep baseline used in the main model comparisons, we set the stimulus component to the smooth firing-rate estimate obtained from the Poisson-GP model. Specifically, we set st,s=σgs2ft,sdt=ft,sdt/rs=λ^t,s/rs\begin{document}$s_{t, s}=\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2} f_{t, s} d t=f_{t, s} d t / r_{s}=\hat{\lambda}_{t, s} / r_{s}$\end{document}, where λ^t,s\begin{document}$\hat{\lambda}_{t, s}$\end{document} denotes the Poisson-GP estimate of the stimulus-dependent firing rate. For s = 1, …, S, we searched for the values of rs that minimize the negative log-likelihood of the spiking observations y1:T,s,1:N. Details of Equation 17, the Fano factor, and the version without a smoothness prior on the stimulus drive are provided in the Appendix 3.

This is another extension of the Goris model [1] to continuous time. Here, we assumed the gain is constant across time bins within a trial:
yt,s,n∼Poisson(λt,s,n)λt,s,n=gs,nft,sdt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} y_{t, s, n} & \sim \operatorname{Poisson}\left(\lambda_{t, s, n}\right) \\ \lambda_{t, s, n} & =g_{s, n} f_{t, s} d t\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where gs,n ~ Gamma(rs, ss) with shape parameter rs=1σgs2\begin{document}$r_{s}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}}$\end{document} and scale parameter ss=σgs2\begin{document}$s_{s}=\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document}. Further, we assumed that the total spike count across all time bins in a trial, Ys,n = ∑t yt,s,n,, also shares the same gain variance σgs2\begin{document}$\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document}, i.e, Ys,n∼Poisson(g~s,nfsdtT)\begin{document}$Y_{s, n} \sim \operatorname{Poisson}\left(\tilde{g}_{s, n} f_{s} d t T\right)$\end{document} with g~s,n∼Gamma(rs,ss)\begin{document}$\tilde{g}_{s, n} \sim \operatorname{Gamma}\left(r_{s}, s_{s}\right)$\end{document}. For the Gorisconst baseline used in the main model comparisons, we estimated σgs2\begin{document}$\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document} by fitting Ys,n across training data n = 1, …, N, as in Goris et al. [1], and set the stimulus encoding component ft,sdt=λ^t,s\begin{document}$f_{t, s} d t=\hat{\lambda}_{t, s}$\end{document}, where λ^t,s\begin{document}$\hat{\lambda}_{t, s}$\end{document} denotes the smooth firing-rate estimate obtained from the Poisson-GP model. Details of the mean-variance relationship, log-likelihood derivation, and the version without a smoothness prior on the stimulus drive are provided in the Appendix 3.

This model follows the same generative structure as the CMP model, except that the gain covariance is parameterized by the standard RBF kernel:


Kg(t1,t2)=ρgexp⁡(−12|tm−tnℓg|2)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)=\rho_{g} \exp \left(-\frac{1}{2}\left|\frac{t_{m}-t_{n}}{\ell_{g}}\right|^{2}\right)$$\end{document}

This variant also follows the CMP framework, with the gain covariance parameterized by a Matérn kernel of order 0 in the CMP-Matérn-0 model:
Kg(t1,t2)=ρgexp⁡(−|t1−t2ℓg|) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)=\rho_{g} \exp \left(-\left|\frac{t_{1}-t_{2}}{\ell_{g}}\right|\right)$$\end{document}
and a Matérn kernel of order 1 in the CMP-Matérn-1 model:
Kg(t1,t2)=ρg(1+3|t1−t2ℓg|)exp⁡(−3|t1−t2ℓg|) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)=\rho_{g}\left(1+\sqrt{3}\left|\frac{t_{1}-t_{2}}{\ell_{g}}\right|\right) \exp \left(-\sqrt{3}\left|\frac{t_{1}-t_{2}}{\ell_{g}}\right|\right)$$\end{document}
We used the same variational inference procedure as the CMP model to infer the hyperparameters and latent variables of the CMP-RBF and CMP-Matérn models. The log-likelihood and mean-variance derivations for these models are analogous to those of the CMP model.

statement.p:
(Covariance function). A (real-valued) function
K:R×R→R \begin{document}$$\displaystyle K: \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$$\end{document}
is called a covariance kernel (or positive semidefinite kernel) if, for every finite set of points t1, …, tn ∈ ℝ and all real weights a1, …, an ∈ ℝ, the following Gram sum is non-negative:
∑i=1n∑j=1naiajK(ti,tj)≥0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{i} a_{j} K\left(t_{i}, t_{j}\right) \geq 0$$\end{document}
We now invoke the following result from the Schoenberg’s theorem [72].

From Lemma 1 and Definition 1, it follows that for any finite set t1,…,tn∈R\begin{document}$t_{1}, \ldots, t_{n} \in \mathbb{R}$\end{document} and weights a1, …, an ∈ ℝ,
∑i=1n∑j=1naiajexp⁡(−|t~i−t~j|q)≥0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{i} a_{j} \exp \left(-\left|\tilde{t}_{i}-\tilde{t}_{j}\right|^{q}\right) \geq 0$$\end{document}
if 0 < q ≤ 2.

Next, consider the change of variables ti=21/qℓg⋅t~i\begin{document}$t_{i}=2^{1 / q} \ell_{g} \cdot \tilde{t}_{i}$\end{document}, which implies:


t~i−t~j|q=|ti(2)1/qℓg−tj(2)1/qℓg|q=12|ti−tjℓg|q
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \tilde{t}_{i}-\left.\tilde{t}_{j}\right|^{q}=\left|\frac{t_{i}}{(2)^{1 / q} \ell_{g}}-\frac{t_{j}}{(2)^{1 / q} \ell_{g}}\right|^{q}=\frac{1}{2}\left|\frac{t_{i}-t_{j}}{\ell_{g}}\right|^{q}$$\end{document}



Substituting this into the above expression, we obtain:


∑i=1n∑j=1naiajexp⁡(−12|ti−tjℓg|q)≥0
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{i} a_{j} \exp \left(-\frac{1}{2}\left|\frac{t_{i}-t_{j}}{\ell_{g}}\right|^{q}\right) \geq 0$$\end{document}



Finally, since ρg > 0, multiplying by ρg preserves non-negativity:


∑i=1n∑j=1naiajρgexp⁡(−12|ti−tjℓg|q)≥0
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{i} a_{j} \rho_{g} \exp \left(-\frac{1}{2}\left|\frac{t_{i}-t_{j}}{\ell_{g}}\right|^{q}\right) \geq 0$$\end{document}



Hence, for any finite set t1, …, tn ∈ ℝ and any real weights a1, …, an ∈ ℝ, we have:


∑i=1n∑j=1naiajKg(ti,tj)≥0
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n} \sum_{j=1}^{n} a_{i} a_{j} K_{g}\left(t_{i}, t_{j}\right) \geq 0$$\end{document}



which establishes that the EPL kernel in Equation 1 is positive semidefinite, and thus a valid covariance function.

app.p:
Recall the CMP forward model:


y(t)∣s∼PoissProc(λ(t)),λ(t)=fs(t)⋅g(t),g~(t):=log⁡g(t)∼GP(0,Kg),Kg(t1,t2)=ρgexp⁡(−12|t1−t2ℓg|q).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} y(t) \mid s & \sim \operatorname{PoissProc}(\lambda(t)), \\ \lambda(t) & =f_{s}(t) \cdot g(t), \\ \tilde{g}(t) & :=\log g(t) \sim \mathcal{G} \mathcal{P}\left(0, K_{g}\right), \\ K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right) & =\rho_{g} \exp \left(-\frac{1}{2}\left|\frac{t_{1}-t_{2}}{\ell_{g}}\right|^{q}\right) .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



Under this model, the mean spike count in a time bin of size Δ, conditioned on the stimulus s, can be derived as:


mean[yΔ∣s]=E[yΔ∣s
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \operatorname{mean}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right.$$\end{document}




(2)

=E[∫0Δy(t)dt∣s]=(a)∫0ΔE[y(t)∣s]dt=(b)∫0ΔE[λ(t)∣s]dt=∫0ΔE[fs(t)⋅g(t)∣s]dt=∫0Δfs(t)E[g(t)]dt=∫0Δfs(t)E[exp⁡(g~(t))]dt=∫0Δfs(t)exp⁡(ρg/2)dt=exp⁡(ρg/2)∫0Δfs(t)dt
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & =\mathbb{E}\left[\int_{0}^{\Delta} y(t) d t \mid s\right] \\ & \stackrel{(a)}{=} \int_{0}^{\Delta} \mathbb{E}[y(t) \mid s] d t \\ & \stackrel{(b)}{=} \int_{0}^{\Delta} \mathbb{E}[\lambda(t) \mid s] d t \\ & =\int_{0}^{\Delta} \mathbb{E}\left[f_{s}(t) \cdot g(t) \mid s\right] d t \\ & =\int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) \mathbb{E}[g(t)] d t \\ & =\int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) \mathbb{E}[\exp (\tilde{g}(t))] d t \\ & =\int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) \exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right) d t \\ & =\exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right) \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t \tag{2} \end{align*}$$\end{document}



where (a) follows from Fubini’s Theorem [73], (b) uses the fact that the mean of a Poisson process equals its rate function [3], and (c) applies the moment-generating function of a Gaussian (i.e., the mean of a log-normal distribution) [74].

Using similar steps, the second moment can be derived as:
(3)
+∬t1≠t2fs(t1)fs(t2)exp⁡(ρg+Kg(t1,t2))dt1dt2=exp⁡(ρg/2)∫0Δfs(t)dt+exp⁡(ρg)∬fs(t1)fs(t2)exp⁡(Kg(t1,t2))dt1dt2 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & \quad+\iint_{t_{1} \neq t_{2}} f_{s}\left(t_{1}\right) f_{s}\left(t_{2}\right) \exp \left(\rho_{g}+K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)\right) d t_{1} d t_{2} \\ & =\exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right) \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t+\exp \left(\rho_{g}\right) \iint f_{s}\left(t_{1}\right) f_{s}\left(t_{2}\right) \exp \left(K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)\right) d t_{1} d t_{2} \tag{3} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Thus, using Equation 2 and Equation 3 the variance can be formulated as:
(4)
var[yΔ∣s]=E[yΔ2∣s]−(E[yΔ∣s])2=exp⁡(ρg/2)∫0Δfs(t)dt+exp⁡(ρg)∬fs(t1)fs(t2)exp⁡(Kg(t1,t2))dt1dt2−(exp⁡(ρg/2)∫0Δfs(t)dt)=exp⁡(ρg/2)∫0Δfs(t)dt+exp⁡(ρg)∬fs(t1)fs(t2)(exp⁡(Kg(t1,t2))−1)dt1dt2 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} \operatorname{var}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right] & =\mathbb{E}\left[y_{\Delta}^{2} \mid s\right]-\left(\mathbb{E}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right]\right)^{2} \\ & =\exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right) \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t+\exp \left(\rho_{g}\right) \iint f_{s}\left(t_{1}\right) f_{s}\left(t_{2}\right) \exp \left(K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)\right) d t_{1} d t_{2} \\ & -\left(\exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right) \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t\right) \\ & =\exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right) \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t+\exp \left(\rho_{g}\right) \iint f_{s}\left(t_{1}\right) f_{s}\left(t_{2}\right)\left(\exp \left(K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)\right)-1\right) d t_{1} d t_{2} \tag{4} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
From these expressions, we can compute Fano factor as a function of bin size, which is simply the ratio of variance (Equation 4) to mean (Equation 2):
FF(Δ)=var[yΔ∣s]E[yΔ∣s]=exp⁡(ρg/2)∫0Δfs(t)dt+exp⁡(ρg)∬fs(t1)fs(t2)(exp⁡(Kg(t1,t2))−1)dt1dt2exp⁡(ρg/2)∫0Δfs(t)dt=1+exp⁡(ρg/2)∫0Δ∫0Δfs(t1)fs(t2)(exp⁡(Kg(t1,t2))−1)dt1dt2∫0Δfs(t)dt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} F F(\Delta) & =\frac{\operatorname{var}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right]}{\mathbb{E}\left[y_{\Delta} \mid s\right]} \\ & =\frac{\exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right) \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t+\exp \left(\rho_{g}\right) \iint f_{s}\left(t_{1}\right) f_{s}\left(t_{2}\right)\left(\exp \left(K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)\right)-1\right) d t_{1} d t_{2}}{\exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right) \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t} \\ & =1+\frac{\exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right) \int_{0}^{\Delta} \int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}\left(t_{1}\right) f_{s}\left(t_{2}\right)\left(\exp \left(K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)\right)-1\right) d t_{1} d t_{2}}{\int_{0}^{\Delta} f_{s}(t) d t} .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
In the special case where the stimulus component is non-fluctuating, i.e., fs(t)=f\begin{document}$f_{s}(t)=f$\end{document}, these expressions simplify to:
and:
FF(Δ)=1+Δλ(κ(Δ)−1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle F F(\Delta)=1+\Delta \lambda(\kappa(\Delta)-1)$$\end{document}
where we define λ=fexp⁡(ρg/2)\begin{document}$\lambda=f \exp \left(\rho_{g} / 2\right)$\end{document} and κ(Δ)=1Δ2∫0Δ∫0ΔeKg(t1,t2)dt1dt2\begin{document}$\kappa(\Delta)=\frac{1}{\Delta^{2}} \int_{0}^{\Delta} \int_{0}^{\Delta} e^{K_{g}\left(t_{1}, t_{2}\right)} d t_{1} d t_{2}$\end{document}.

sec.p:
Here we provide further details on the Goris-independent gain model, including the derivation of the likelihood, as well as the mean and variance under this model. The Goris-indep model is an extension of the modulated Poisson model in Goris et al. [1], adapted for spike train data with multiple time bins per trial. Given spiking observations of the sth stimulus presentation across t = 1, …, T time bins, we assume that the modulatory gain process is independent across time bins, with shared variance σgs2\begin{document}$\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document}, i.e.,
yt,s,n∼Poisson(λt,s,n),λt,s,n=gt,s,nft,sdt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} y_{t, s, n} & \sim \operatorname{Poisson}\left(\lambda_{t, s, n}\right), \\ \lambda_{t, s, n} & =g_{t, s, n} f_{t, s} d t,\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where gt,s,n ~ Gamma(rs, ss), with shape parameter rs=1σgs2\begin{document}$r_{s}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}}$\end{document} and scale parameter ss=σgs2\begin{document}$s_{s}=\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document}. This implies λt,s,n ~ Gamma (rs, st,s), where st,s=σgs2ft,sdt\begin{document}$s_{t, s}=\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2} f_{t, s} d t$\end{document}.

Based on the above derivation, we see that:


p(yt,s,n|rs,st,s)∼NegativeBinomial(rs,11+st,s)

\begin{document}$$\displaystyle p \left( y_{t,s,n} \mid r_s, s_{t,s} \right) \sim \mathrm{NegativeBinomial} \left(r_{s}, \frac{1}{1+s_{t, s}}\right)$$\end{document}



and y1:t,s,n are independent across n = 1, …, N when conditioned on rs and st,s. Thus, from the mean and variance of the Negative Binomial distribution, we have:


mean[yt,s,1:N∣ft,s,dt]=E[yt,s,n∣ft,s,dt]=rsst,s=ft,sdtvar[yt,s,1:N∣ft,s,dt]=rsst,s(1+st,s)=ft,sdt(1+σgs2ft,sdt).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \operatorname{mean}\left[y_{t, s, 1: N} \mid f_{t, s}, d t\right] & =\mathbb{E}\left[y_{t, s, n} \mid f_{t, s}, d t\right]=r_{s} s_{t, s}=f_{t, s} d t \\ \operatorname{var}\left[y_{t, s, 1: N} \mid f_{t, s}, d t\right] & =r_{s} s_{t, s}\left(1+s_{t, s}\right)=f_{t, s} d t\left(1+\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2} f_{t, s} d t\right) .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



Next, we extend this to derive the mean and variance of spike counts over a larger time window Δ = R dt, by summing across the relevant bins t1, …, tR:


mean(yΔ,s,1:N∣fs,dt)=E[yΔ,s,n∣fs,dt]=E[∑r′=1Rytr′,s,n∣fs,dt]=∑r′=1RE[ytr′,s,n∣fs,dt]=∑r′=1Rrsstr′,s
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \operatorname{mean}\left(y_{\Delta, s, 1: N} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right)=\mathbb{E}\left[y_{\Delta, s, n} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] & =\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} y_{t_{r^{\prime}}, s, n} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] \\ & =\sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} \mathbb{E}\left[y_{t_{r^{\prime}, s, n}} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] \\ & =\sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} r_{s} s_{t_{r^{\prime}, s}}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}





=∑r′=1Rftr′,sdt
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle =\sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} f_{t_{r^{\prime}, s}} d t$$\end{document}



and:


var[yΔ,s,1:N∣fs,dt]=var[∑r′=1Rytr′,s,n∣fs,dt]=∑r′=1Rvar[ytr′,s,n∣fs,dt]=∑r′=1Rrsstr′,s(1+str′,s)=∑r′=1Rftr′,sdt(1+σgs2ftr′,sdt)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \operatorname{var}\left[y_{\Delta, s, 1: N} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] & =\operatorname{var}\left[\sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} y_{t_{r^{\prime}}, s, n} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] \\ & =\sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} \operatorname{var}\left[y_{t_{r^{\prime}, s, n}} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] \\ & =\sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} r_{s} s_{t_{r^{\prime}, s}}\left(1+s_{t_{r^{\prime}, s}}\right) \\ & =\sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} f_{t_{r^{\prime}, s}} d t\left(1+\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2} f_{t_{r^{\prime}, s}} d t\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}

In this section, we provide further details on the Goris-constant gain model, including the derivation of the mean and variance, as well as the log-likelihood under its modeling assumptions. The Goris-const model is the second variant we consider for extending the Goris model in [1] to multiple time bins. Here, we assume that the modulatory gain process is constant across time bins:
yt,s,n∼Poisson(λt,s,n)λt,s,n=gs,nft,sdt, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & y_{t, s, n} \sim \operatorname{Poisson}\left(\lambda_{t, s, n}\right) \\ & \lambda_{t, s, n}=g_{s, n} f_{t, s} d t,\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
where gs,n ~ Gamma(rs, ss), with shape parameter rs=1σgs2\begin{document}$r_{s}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}}$\end{document} and scale parameter ss=σgs2\begin{document}$s_{s}=\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document}. Furthermore, we assume that the total spike count across all time bins in a trial, Ys,n = ∑t yt,s,n, shares the same gain variance σgs2\begin{document}$\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document}, i.e.,
(6)
Ys,n∼Poisson(g~s,nfsT), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} Y_{s, n} \sim \operatorname{Poisson}\left(\tilde{g}_{s, n} f_{s} T\right), \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
with g~s,n∼Gamma(rs,ss)\begin{document}$\tilde{g}_{s, n} \sim \operatorname{Gamma}\left(r_{s}, s_{s}\right)$\end{document}. For the Goris-const model used in the main model comparisons, we estimate σgs2\begin{document}$\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}$\end{document} by fitting the total spike counts Ys,n across training trials, as in Goris et al. [1], and set the stimulus encoding component ft,sdt=λ^t,s\begin{document}$f_{t, s} d t=\hat{\lambda}_{t, s}$\end{document}, where λ^t,s\begin{document}$\hat{\lambda}_{t, s}$\end{document} denotes the smooth firing-rate estimate obtained from the Poisson-GP model. For the comparison without a smoothness prior on the stimulus drive, we instead estimate ft,sdt using the same method as in the baseline Poisson model.

Note that from the moments of the Gamma distribution:
(7)
E[gs,n]=1,E[gs,n2]=σgs2+1. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbb{E}\left[g_{s, n}\right]=1, \quad \mathbb{E}\left[g_{s, n}^{2}\right]=\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}+1 . \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Accordingly, we derive the first moment, second moment, and variance at bin size dt as:
We then extend these results to a larger bin size Δ = R dt, by integrating over the differential statistics of the corresponding time bins t1, · · ·, tR at bin-size dt:
mean(yΔ,s,1:N∣fs,dt)=E[yΔ,s,n∣fs,dt]=E[∑r=1Rytr,s,n∣fs,dt]=∑r=1RE[ytr,s,n∣fs,dt]=∑r=1Rftr,sdt,E[yΔ,s,n2∣fs,dt]=E[(∑r=1Rytr,s,n)2∣fs,dt]]=E[∑r=1R∑r′=1Rytr,s,nytr′,s,n∣fs,dt]=∑r=1RE[ytr,s,n2∣fs,dt]+∑r≠r′E[ytr,s,nytr′,s,n∣fs,dt]=∑r=1R(ftr,sdt+ftr,s2dt2(σgs2+1))+∑r≠r′(ftr,sftr′,sdt2(σgs2+1))=∑r=1Rftr,sdt+∑r=1R∑r′=1R(ftr,sftr′,sdt2(σgs2+1)), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \text { mean }\left(y_{\Delta, s, 1: N} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right)=\mathbb{E}\left[y_{\Delta, s, n} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] & =\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{r=1}^{R} y_{t_{r}, s, n} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] \\ & =\sum_{r=1}^{R} \mathbb{E}\left[y_{t_{r}, s, n} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] \\ & =\sum_{r=1}^{R} f_{t_{r}, s} d t, \\ \mathbb{E}\left[y_{\Delta, s, n}^{2} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] & \left.=\mathbb{E}\left[\left(\sum_{r=1}^{R} y_{t_{r}, s, n}\right)^{2} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] \quad\right] \quad \\ & =\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{r=1}^{R} \sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R} y_{t_{r}, s, n} y_{t_{r^{\prime}}, s, n} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] \\ & =\sum_{r=1}^{R} \mathbb{E}\left[y_{t_{r}, s, n}^{2} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right]+\sum_{r \neq r^{\prime}} \mathbb{E}\left[y_{t_{r}, s, n} y_{t_{r^{\prime}}, s, n} \mid \mathbf{f}_{\mathbf{s}}, d t\right] \\ & =\sum_{r=1}^{R}\left(f_{t_{r}, s} d t+f_{t_{r}, s}^{2} d t^{2}\left(\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}+1\right)\right)+\sum_{r \neq r^{\prime}}\left(f_{t_{r}, s} f_{t_{r^{\prime}}, s} d t^{2}\left(\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}+1\right)\right) \\ & =\sum_{r=1}^{R} f_{t_{r}, s} d t+\sum_{r=1}^{R} \sum_{r^{\prime}=1}^{R}\left(f_{t_{r}, s} f_{t_{r^{\prime}}, s} d t^{2}\left(\sigma_{g_{s}}^{2}+1\right)\right),\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
